    "exercisePages": "141-142",
    "exerciseTitle": "Use it on the drills.",
    "exerciseIntro": "The book has one drill: read Quranic phrases aloud (pp. 141-142). Split here into six passes, one per mark. Readings end in waqf.",
    "exerciseGuide": [
      {
        "exercise": 1,
        "pages": "141",
        "task": "Read alif waṣl ٱ at the start and in the middle",
        "method": "First word of the phrase: pronounce ٱ (ʔa, or ʔi as in ٱِعۡتَمَرَ). Anything before it: drop ٱ and link the previous vowel to the next sound.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "بِٱسۡمِ رَبِّكَ",
          "translit": "bis mi rab bik",
          "note": "ٱ dropped after بِ"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "ٱلرَّحۡمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ",
          "translit": "ʔar raḥ maa nir ra ḥiim",
          "note": "1st ٱ said, 2nd dropped"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 2,
        "pages": "141",
        "task": "Decide whether the laam after ٱ is said",
        "method": "لۡ (sukuun mark) = moon laam, say it. Bare laam + shaddah on the next letter = sun laam: skip the laam, double the next letter (Chapter 6).",
        "example": {
          "ar": "وَٱلۡقَمَرِ",
          "translit": "wal qa mar",
          "note": "لۡ -> said"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "وَٱلشَّمۡسِ",
          "translit": "wash shams",
          "note": "bare laam -> silent"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 3,
        "pages": "141-142",
        "task": "Turn every dagger alif into a long aa",
        "method": "Small vertical alif = long aa. In ٱلۡحَيَوٰةِ, وٰ replaces a regular alif: read ḥa yaa, without a w sound. In زَكَّىٰهَا, ىٰ gives long aa. A waaw with fatḥah plus dagger alif, as in ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ, is pronounced waa.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "ٱلۡحَيَوٰةِ ٱلدُّنۡيَا",
          "translit": "ʔal ḥa yaa tid dun yaa",
          "note": "وٰ = aa, not w"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "زَكَّىٰهَا",
          "translit": "zak kaa haa",
          "note": "ىٰ = aa"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 4,
        "pages": "141-142",
        "task": "Say or drop the small waaw and small yaaʔ",
        "method": "Inside a word: always say uu / ii. At the end of a word: say it when you keep reading (waṣl), drop it when you stop (waqf).",
        "example": {
          "ar": "عَنۡ عِبَادِهِۦ وَيَأۡخُذُ",
          "translit": "ʿan ʿi baa di hii wa yaʔ khudh",
          "note": "waṣl -> hii"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "فَلۡيَدۡعُ نَادِيَهُۥ",
          "translit": "fal yad ʿu naa di yah",
          "note": "waqf -> dropped"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 5,
        "pages": "141",
        "task": "Read sukuun: the head-shaped mark and bare letters",
        "method": "A letter with the head-shaped mark, or with no mark at all, has no vowel: close the syllable. A bare ا / و / ي after its matching vowel is a long vowel.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "ٱلۡمَنفُوشِ",
          "translit": "ʔal man fuush",
          "note": "bare ن = sukuun"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "وَٱلۡفَجۡرِ",
          "translit": "wal fajr",
          "note": "sukuun on laam and jiim"
        }
      },
      {
        "exercise": 6,
        "pages": "142",
        "task": "Skip silent letters, hold the madd",
        "method": "A letter with the round zero is not read at all. A long vowel with the wavy madd sign is held about twice as long.",
        "example": {
          "ar": "ءَامَنُوا۟",
          "translit": "ʔaa ma nuu",
          "note": "final ا۟ silent"
        },
        "contrast": {
          "ar": "أَوۡلِيَآءَ",
          "translit": "ʔaw li yaaaa ʔ",
          "note": "يَآ -> hold aa"
        }
      }
    ],
    "exerciseFooter": "**Pause signs:** the small letters above the line in ٱلۡأَرۡضِۚ or يَشَآءُۗ (p. 142) are not letters. The book does not teach them here: do not pronounce them."
